<BaseLayout title="Curved Conveyors: Roller Curves, Belt Curves & Modular Belt Turns" description="Curved conveyors: tapered roller curves, belt curves, modular belt turns, gravity curves, product orientation, and minimum radius for packaging and distribution." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/curved-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Curved Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A curved conveyor changes product travel direction through an angle, typically 30, 45, 90, or 180 degrees, using tapered rollers, curved belt sections, or modular belt designs. Curved conveyors connect straight conveyor runs that cannot align in a single line due to facility layout constraints, column obstructions, or process equipment placement. They maintain continuous product flow around corners in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>, distribution centers, and manufacturing plants where right-angle transfers or turntables would disrupt throughput.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Curved Conveyors Work</h2>
<p>The fundamental challenge of a curved conveyor is differential speed across the belt or roller width. On a curve, the outside edge travels a longer distance than the inside edge. A 90-degree curve with a 36-inch inside radius and a 24-inch belt width has an outside radius of 60 inches. The outside edge travels about 94 inches (60 x pi/2) while the inside edge travels about 57 inches (36 x pi/2), so the outside path is about 67% longer. The conveyor must accommodate this speed differential or products rotate, skew, or jam.</p>
<p>Different curved conveyor technologies address this differential speed problem in different ways. Tapered rollers spin faster at the outside edge than the inside edge. Curved belts use conical or crowned tracking rollers to maintain belt position while allowing the belt to flex through the curve. <a href="/resources/conveyor-belting/">Modular plastic belts</a> articulate at each hinge point to accommodate the differential path length inherently.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/curve-fmh-belt-curve-parcel.webp" width="1000" height="561" alt="A black curved belt conveyor in an aluminum frame turns product flow between straight roller conveyor sections in a distribution facility." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A curved belt conveyor joining straight conveyor runs that meet at an angle. The continuous belt carries product through the turn without the gaps of a roller curve. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://fmhconveyors.com/products/curved-belt-conveyors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FMH Conveyors</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Curve Geometry</h3>
<p>Curved conveyors are defined by three dimensions: curve angle (degrees of turn), inside radius (distance from the curve center to the inside belt or roller edge), and belt or roller width. Common catalog angles are 30, 45, 60, and 90 degrees (Hytrol's <a href="https://www.cisco-eagle.com/uploads/Hytrol-Spec-Gravity/GSRCT-specs.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Model GSRCT gravity roller curve specification</a> lists these four), and 180-degree turns are built from two 90-degree sections or as one unit. Custom angles are available from many manufacturers. The inside radius determines the curve footprint and the severity of the differential speed issue, tighter radii create larger speed differentials. Wider belts on tight curves experience the greatest differential and are the most challenging to manage.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Types of Curved Conveyors</h2>
<h3>Tapered Roller Curves</h3>
<p>Tapered roller curves use conical (tapered) rollers that are wider at the outside edge of the curve than at the inside edge. The taper causes the roller surface to move faster at the outside than at the inside, matching the differential path length of the curve. Products ride on these tapered rollers and change direction smoothly without rotating. Tapered rollers are the standard solution for <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> curves handling cases, cartons, and totes. Powered tapered roller curves use a belt or chain drive to rotate the rollers, while gravity tapered roller curves rely on product weight and momentum.</p>
<p>The taper ratio depends on the curve geometry, tighter curves with wider conveyors need more aggressive taper. Tapered roller curves are sold in 30 through 90 degree sections and combined for larger turns. Hytrol's GSRCT tapered gravity curves, for example, list a 32-1/2 inch inside radius for 16 to 30 inch overall widths and 48 inches for 34 to 42 inch widths. Product size sets the practical limit: Hytrol's training manual calls for a 2 inch clearance between the package corners and the outside radius, which may require a curve wider than the straight sections.</p>
<h3>Belt Curves</h3>
<p>Belt curves use a continuous belt that flexes through the curve over a curved slider bed or curved roller bed. The belt tracks on the curve using conical end rollers (drive and tail rollers with a taper that matches the curve geometry) and lateral belt guides. Belt curves provide a smooth, continuous surface for products, no gaps between rollers, making them suitable for small items, bags, and irregularly shaped products that would catch on roller gaps.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/curve-interroll-belt-curve.webp" width="1000" height="563" alt="Product image of a 90-degree belt curve with a black belt, black side frames, low side guards, and a gearmotor mounted at one end." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A 90-degree belt curve module. The belt forms a fan shape, wider at the outside radius, and the drive sits at one end of the curve. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.interroll.com/products/belt-curves" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Interroll</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/flat-belt-conveyors/">Flat belt</a> curves handle the differential speed by allowing the belt to slide laterally on the slider bed. The inside edge of the belt decelerates slightly while the outside edge accelerates, accommodated by the belt elasticity and the conical rollers at each end. This introduces some belt wear and requires periodic tracking adjustment. Belt curves are noisier and consume more power than straight belt runs due to the increased friction.</p>
<h3>Modular Belt Curves</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">Modular belt</a> conveyors navigate curves inherently. The interlocking plastic modules articulate at each hinge point, allowing inside modules to compress together while outside modules spread apart. No special curve-specific hardware is needed, the same modular belt that handles straight runs handles curves. This eliminates the belt tracking challenges of fabric belt curves and the product gaps of roller curves. Minimum turn radius depends on module design and is stated as a multiple of belt width, measured from the inside edge. Intralox rates its <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-2200/radius-flush-grid" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Series 2200 Radius Flush Grid</a> belt at 2.2 times belt width and its <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-2300/flush-grid-nose-roller-tight-turning" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Series 2300 tight-turning</a> belt at 1.7 times belt width for widths up to 27 inches.</p>
<h3>Gravity Curves</h3>
<p>Gravity curves use unpowered tapered rollers or skate wheels on a curved frame. Products coast through the curve under their own momentum from the upstream powered conveyor section. Gravity curves are the simplest curve option: no motor, drive train, or controls. They work for rigid, flat-bottomed products (cases, cartons, totes) with sufficient momentum to carry through the curve without stalling. Lightweight products, bags, and flexible items stall on gravity curves because they lack the mass and momentum to overcome roller friction through the turn. Hytrol recommends a guard rail on the outside radius of nearly every curve to keep boxes from falling off.</p>
<h3>Wheel Curves</h3>
<p>Wheel curves (skate wheel curves) use rows of small skate wheels mounted on axles that follow the curve radius. The wheels provide low-friction product support with minimal contact area. Wheel curves suit flat-bottomed rigid products and have few wearing parts. Hytrol notes that skatewheel curves, like tapered roller curves, help boxes stay oriented. They do not suit products with soft bottoms, small footprints, or irregular shapes because these products sag between the wheel rows or catch on wheel edges. Wheel curves are normally gravity-only.</p>
<h2 id="orient-heading">Product Orientation Through Curves</h2>
<p>Product rotation through curves is a common concern. On a curve, differential speed between the inside and outside edges creates a torque on the product that tends to rotate it. The degree of rotation depends on the curve radius (tighter curves cause more rotation), the product aspect ratio (long narrow products rotate more than square products), and the friction between the product bottom and the conveyor surface.</p>
<p><strong>Tapered rollers minimize rotation</strong> by matching the surface speed differential to the curve geometry. When the taper ratio is correct, every point across the roller width moves at the speed appropriate for its distance from the curve center. Products ride through the curve without rotation because no net torque acts on them. Mismatched taper ratios (wrong rollers for the curve radius) cause rotation in one direction or the other.</p>
<p><strong>Side guides control product position</strong> through curves but introduce friction. Inside guides prevent products from cutting the corner and falling off the inside edge. Outside guards prevent products from flying off the outside edge due to centrifugal effects. Adjustable guides accommodate different product widths. Spring-loaded or roller-edge guides reduce friction and prevent product damage on contact.</p>
<p><strong>Product spacing through curves</strong> must increase compared to straight runs. Products on a curve occupy more conveyor width because they take a diagonal position as they rotate through the turn. If products are spaced too closely, adjacent products contact each other on the curve, causing jams. Hytrol's <a href="https://www.cisco-eagle.com/uploads/Hytrol-Literature/Basic-Conveyor-Training.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Basic Training Manual</a> notes that boxes traveling through a curve in a touching train may not stay oriented, and that most of a package must be on the curve before it starts to turn. Gap products ahead of the curve and confirm the spacing with real product.</p>
<p><strong>High-speed curves</strong> amplify rotation and centrifugal problems. Products traveling fast through tight curves can slide outward, tip over, or rotate. Hytrol notes that boxes moving at high speed will not negotiate a gravity curve. Reducing speed through curves, using a separate drive zone with a slower belt or roller speed, improves product stability. Alternatively, increasing the curve radius reduces the centrifugal force and differential speed for a given belt speed.</p>
<h2 id="power-heading">Powered vs. Gravity Curves</h2>
<p><strong>Powered curves</strong> use motor-driven rollers or belts to actively propel products through the turn. The drive ensures consistent product speed and reliable transport regardless of product weight or momentum. Powered curves are essential when products vary in weight, when the upstream conveyor speed is low (insufficient momentum for gravity), when the curve leads to an incline, or when precise product spacing must be maintained through the turn.</p>
<p>Drive methods for powered roller curves include tangential belt drives (a flat belt running along the underside of the rollers, driving them through friction), round belt or O-ring drives (round belts connecting adjacent rollers through grooves), and motorized rollers (one or more rollers with integral motors that drive adjacent rollers through belts or gears). MDR (motor-driven roller) curve sections integrate directly with <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR straight sections</a> for zone-controlled curve operation, using the same low-voltage DC motorized rollers and zone controls as the straight sections.</p>
<p><strong>Gravity curves</strong> cost less and require no power or controls. They suit applications where products carry sufficient momentum from upstream powered sections, product weight is relatively consistent, and the curve angle is 90 degrees or less. Gravity curves at 180 degrees are challenging because products lose too much momentum through the long turn. Declining a gravity curve (dropping it along the direction of travel) adds gravity assist, but the frame must be twisted to do it. Hytrol permits a slight twist up to a 6 inch decline for a 90-degree curve.</p>
<p><strong>Hybrid approaches</strong> use gravity curves with powered assist at the entry or exit. A short powered section at the curve entry boosts product momentum into the gravity curve. A powered section at the curve exit pulls products out of the curve and onto the downstream conveyor. This hybrid approach avoids powering the curve itself while handling the momentum limits of pure gravity operation. Hytrol recommends a straight section at least as long as the longest package ahead of both gravity and powered curves.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Curved Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Packaging line direction changes:</strong> Curved conveyors redirect product flow around obstacles, between process equipment, and through facility constraints. A typical packaging line includes multiple 90-degree curves to fit within available floor space while connecting fillers, labelers, case packers, and palletizers in a logical process sequence.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution center sortation layouts:</strong> High-speed curved conveyors connect straight sortation lanes in distribution and fulfillment centers. Products travel through multiple curves at high speed between induction points, scanning stations, and divert locations. These curves use powered tapered rollers or belt curves to maintain product orientation and spacing at high speeds.</p>
<p><strong>U-turn and return conveyors:</strong> 180-degree curves create U-turn conveyors that reverse product direction. Return conveyors bring empty containers, pallets, or trays back to the line start using 180-degree curves at each end. Food processing lines use 180-degree curves on conveyor accumulation tables (serpentine layouts) to maximize buffering capacity in compact floor space.</p>
<p><strong>Mezzanine and multi-level layouts:</strong> Curved conveyors at mezzanine levels navigate around structural columns, stairways, and equipment while maintaining product flow between vertical transport points (spiral conveyors, VRCs, <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">incline conveyors</a>) and processing or storage areas.</p>
<p><strong>Airport baggage handling:</strong> Curved conveyor sections route luggage through the complex paths between check-in, security screening, sorting, and aircraft loading. These curves handle high speeds, mixed product sizes (luggage varies greatly), and continuous high-volume operation. The curves use powered belt or powered roller designs for reliable transport of irregularly shaped, varying-weight bags.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Curved Conveyor</h2>
<p><strong>Define the curve angle and available space.</strong> The facility layout determines the required turn angle and the available floor area for the curve. Tighter curves (smaller radius) save floor space but increase product handling challenges. Leave room for maintenance access around the curve, both inside and outside radii need clearance for roller replacement and belt adjustment. Drive belts, chains, and tapered roller nip points under the curve need guarding under <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">29 CFR 1910.212</a> and <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Match the curve type to the product.</strong> Rigid flat-bottomed products (cases, cartons, totes): tapered roller curve or gravity wheel curve. Small items, bags, pouches: belt curve. Products requiring washdown: modular belt curve. Mixed product sizes with varying weights: powered tapered roller curve.</p>
<p><strong>Size the radius for the product diagonal.</strong> The product's diagonal dimension (corner to corner) must fit within the belt or roller width throughout the curve. On the inside of the curve, the effective conveyor width narrows because the product sweeps an arc wider than its own width. Calculate the required conveyor width based on the product diagonal and the curve radius using the geometry of the curve arc.</p>
<p><strong>Decide powered or gravity.</strong> Use powered curves when product weight varies widely, when upstream speed is too low to carry product through the turn, when the curve angle exceeds 90 degrees, or when precise product spacing is critical. Use gravity curves to avoid drive and control cost when product weight is consistent, momentum is sufficient, and spacing precision is not critical. Test with the lightest and heaviest real products.</p>
