<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a curved conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A curved conveyor changes product direction through an angle (30, 45, 90, or 180 degrees) using tapered rollers, curved belts, or modular belt designs. It maintains continuous product flow around corners in packaging, distribution, and manufacturing layouts. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor guide</a> for tapered roller details.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the minimum radius for a curved conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on type, width, and product. Hytrol's GSRCT tapered gravity roller curves list a 32-1/2 inch inside radius for 16 to 30 inch overall widths and 48 inches for 34 to 42 inch widths. Intralox rates its Series 2200 radius <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">modular belts</a> at a minimum turn radius of 2.2 times belt width, measured from the inside edge, and its Series 2300 tight-turning belt at 1.7 times belt width for widths up to 27 inches. Belt curve radii vary by manufacturer. Hytrol also calls for 2 inches of clearance between package corners and the outside radius, which can require a curve wider than the straight sections.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Do products rotate when going through a curved conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Products tend to rotate because the outside edge travels faster than the inside edge. Tapered roller curves minimize rotation by matching the speed differential across the curve width. Proper taper ratio, side guides, and increased product spacing all reduce rotation. Tall, narrow products rotate more than wide, flat products.</p>

</div>
</div>
